# TouchDesigner Interactive Art Rules

## Who This Is For
- Jevon Owen-Kennedy learning TouchDesigner while building interactive art
- Practical, ELI5 help — keep it simple, show the "why," don't dump jargon

## How Agents Should Talk About Networks
- Explain operator chains like a story: what goes in → what changes → what comes out
- Prefer plain names: "this node makes the image wiggle" over "SOP/TOP transform cascade"
- When you must use TD terms (TOP, CHOP, SOP, DAT, COMP), define them in one short line first
- Point to specific nodes/paths in the project when possible
- One concept at a time; offer a tiny next experiment after each explanation

## Concepts to Teach (as they come up)
- Operator families: TOP (images), CHOP (numbers/signals), SOP (geometry), DAT (text/tables), COMP (containers)
- Wiring, cooking, and why order/performance matter
- Parameters vs. expressions vs. Python
- Interaction inputs: mouse, keyboard, MIDI, audio, camera, sensors
- Timing: `absTime`, timelines, beat/synced motion
- Reusable pieces: Components (`.tox`) vs. full projects (`.toe`)
- Perform Mode vs. Designer Mode for showing work

## File Organization
- One interactive art piece per subfolder under this directory
- Prefer this layout inside each piece:
  - `README.md` — what the piece is and how to open/run it
  - `project.toe` (or a clear name) — main TouchDesigner project
  - `tox/` — reusable components (`.tox`)
  - `assets/` — media (images, audio, video) used by the network
  - `notes/` — learning notes, sketches, "what I tried"
  - `exports/` — renders, recordings, stills for sharing
- Keep networks tidy: name operators clearly; avoid giant unnamed spaghetti
- Don't commit huge media dumps or TouchDesigner crash dumps unless needed

## Learning Notes
- Capture "aha" moments in `notes/` or check off items in `PROGRESS.md`
- Prefer short notes: goal → what you built → what broke → what fixed it
- When teaching, link the lesson back to the current art piece when possible

## Safety: Destructive Edits
- Never wipe or overwrite a `.toe` / `.tox` without an explicit backup plan
- Before big rewires: suggest Save As / dated copy (e.g. `project_2026-03-26_backup.toe`)
- Prefer additive changes: new nodes beside old ones, then swap wires
- Don't delete operators, external files, or assets unless the user asked
- Call out risky steps clearly ("this replaces your current render chain")
- If unsure what a network does, ask or explain first — don't "clean up" blindly

## Project Style
- Interactive art first: audience can affect something (sound, motion, color, structure)
- Start small; ship a working loop before adding features
- Match the learner's pace — depth on request, not a textbook dump
